Package: infiltratoros
Version: @VERSION@
Section: misc
Priority: optional
Architecture: amd64
Maintainer: Infiltrator Projects <noreply@infiltrator-projects.invalid>
Pre-Depends: libgtk-3-0t64 | libgtk-3-0, fontconfig, util-linux
Depends: plymouth, apt, wget, ca-certificates, bash, binutils, coreutils, libcurl4t64 | libcurl4, libjson-c5, sbsigntool
Description: InfiltratorOS branding and native application integration
 Installs the canonical InfiltratorOS Plymouth splash for Debian and
 Linux Mint derived systems, selects it as the active theme, rebuilds
 initramfs, and replaces distribution System Monitor and Calculator packages
 with Infiltrator builds compiled aggressively for the local CPU.
 .
 BOOT is compiled locally by its independent native installer after the
 selected filesystem providers complete. It installs its EFI executable and
 transitional firmware drivers in the fixed BOOT directory, using Shim when
 Secure Boot is enabled. GRUB remains an independent fallback. The separate
 InfiltratorOS kernel is not installed by this integration package.
 .
 Calendar is compiled locally through its independently published native
 installer. Once available, a backed-up per-user Cinnamon login migration
 swaps the stock calendar applet's panel entry for the Infiltrator applet
 without removing the Cinnamon package or altering any other panel items.
 .
 Files is installed as an additional native file manager in /Programs and is
 compiled with the same hardware-native policy. InfiltratorOS does not purge
 the existing distribution file manager or change the current default handler.
 .
 Defragmenter is added through its checksum-verified native compiler with
 CPU-native -O3 and LTO code generation. No other filesystem utilities are
 removed and its user-facing programs and private engines occupy the native /Programs and
 /System hierarchy.
 .
 The operating system downloads the latest source installer from each of the
 31 calendar and 23 clock repositories. Each module is built locally with
 aggressive CPU-native optimization, LTO and measured two-pass PGO, checked
 and installed as its own Debian package in the system resource folders.
 Removing a consumer does not remove these independent implementations.
 .
 Five established filesystem modules are installed by their own
 checksum-verified CPU-native source packages, with distinct FAT12.so,
 FAT16.so, FAT32.so, OFS.so and exFAT.so ownership under /System/Filesystems. Up to 18 other independent
 filesystem modules may be installed when their native releases qualify.
 Kernel modules and EFI drivers remain independently published, optional
 provider artifacts. System-package mode installs each CPU-native SO and its
 illustrated PDF; it does not automatically build or stage kernel modules.
 .
 Up to 16 eligible native source builds run concurrently within CPU,
 memory and storage limits. Package transactions are serialised.
